---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const demo = "https://cairn.abalazade.workers.dev";
---

<Base
  title="CAIRN: a scroll-driven product page"
  description="A demo store for a made-up flask brand: one scroll position drives a 3D product page, and the same page has a working bag."
  path="/work/cairn"
>
  <a class="back" href="/">Index</a>

  <h1 class="lede">CAIRN</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    A made-up flask brand. Scrolling drives a 3D product page, and the same page has a working bag.
  </p>

  <p><a class="action" href={demo}>Open the demo</a></p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why it's built this way</h2>
    <p>
      Scroll-driven product pages usually run several animations and fight to keep them in sync.
      Stop halfway and the camera says one thing while the text says another. Here everything reads
      from a single number, so that can't happen.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One timeline.</strong> Scroll position becomes one number: the section index plus
      progress through it. Camera, exploded view, spin, scale, text fade and temperatures all come
      from it, so nothing jumps between sections.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The flask is geometry.</strong> Four lathe shapes, a torus for the seal, and a canvas
      gradient used as the lighting environment. No model file and no images to download.
      Switching colour sweeps the key light across it, so it looks relit, not repainted.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The cooling section is a real model.</strong> Newton's law of cooling, run live for
      the flask and for a plain bottle. Because it's a function, a test can check it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The shop rules are tested.</strong> Stock per colour, quantity capped at what's left
      (counting what's already in the bag), and sold-out colours crossed out instead of hidden.
      Thirty-nine assertions cover it, in the browser or in Node.
    </p>
    <p>
      With reduced motion on, you get a static layout with the same content and bag. There's no
      build step: three.js comes from a CDN and the rest is plain files.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
